# Dubizzle Car Scraper

Scrapes used-car listings from `https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/`, stores
them in SQLite via Prisma, and rebuilds the static `index.html` front end.

## Daily use

```bash
npm run scrape        # scrape -> save to db -> reclaim -> export -> rebuild index.html
```

Individual steps:

| command | what it does |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run scrape` | full pipeline (below) |
| `npm run scrape:count` | scrape but only report counts, write nothing (`--dry-run`) |
| `npm run scrape:test` | unit tests + a live Algolia smoke test |
| `npm run test:scrape` | unit tests only, no network |
| `npm run test:db` | save-to-db regression test (throwaway database) |
| `npm run scrape:repair -- <file.json>` | fix schema-invalid fields in an existing scrape file |
| `npm run db:save` | import `Scraper/unprocessed/*.json` into the DB || `npm run export:all` | dump every car from the DB to `all-cars-from-db.json` |
| `npm run sync:index` | rebuild `index.html` from `bootstrap.html` + the export |
| `npm run migrate:template` | convert a `bootstrap.html` that still has inline JSON |
| `npm run studio` | Prisma Studio |

---

## Why the scraper was rewritten

The previous scraper drove a headless browser to
`https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/` and parsed
`#__NEXT_DATA__`. Two things killed it:

1. **Imperva / Incapsula now challenges the requests.** Every request to
   `uae.dubizzle.com` gets a `200` containing a *"Pardon Our Interruption"* page
   plus an `_Incapsula_Resource` iframe and an **hCaptcha** challenge, instead of
   the listing HTML. This is triggered by IP/bot scoring, so it hits headless
   browsers *and* plain HTTP, and it is not fixable with headers or user agents.
   You can see it live: `curl https://uae.dubizzle.com/robots.txt` works (that one
   path is served by `Server: Dubizzle`), while every other path returns the
   challenge.
2. **The page no longer carries the data where the old code looked.** The listing
   page's payload moved: the redux action the scraper read
   (`listings/fetchListingDataForQuery/fulfilled` → `.payload.hits`) is not always
   populated the same way, and the scraper's card selectors had drifted
   (`listing-location` → `.mui-style-t0mppt` is gone). That is why recent scrapes
   wrote tens of thousands of rows with `price`, `make`, `model`, `mileage`,
   `spec` all `null`.

### The new approach

**The listing page is only a shell - it fetches its data from Algolia.** The page
payload ships the credentials it uses:

```
algolia_app_id:  WD0PTZ13ZS
algolia_app_key: cdd839b4fdac840289e88633779e8634   (public search-only key)
algoliaIndexName: motors.com
```

Algolia is a third-party search service, so querying it directly **does not touch
Dubizzle's WAF and never shows a captcha**. The new scraper:

- posts to `https://WD0PTZ13ZS-dsn.algolia.net/1/indexes/motors.com/query`
- filters `category_v2.slug_paths:"motors/used-cars"` (~34.5k live listings)
- pages 1,000 hits at a time
- **partitions** the category by make (and by year when needed) because a single
  Algolia query can only reach the first 10,000 hits

It is also *better data* than before: price, mileage, year and the full spec sheet
arrive as structured fields rather than parsed DOM, and each listing carries all
of its photos.

Measured on a full run: **~34,500 cars in ~3 minutes**, with 100% coverage of
`price`, `mileage`, `year`, `make`, `model`, `detailPageUrl` and `added`.

---

## Options

```bash
node Scraper/scraper.js --limit 200      # smoke test (stops early)
node Scraper/scraper.js --out tmp.json   # custom output file
node Scraper/scraper.js --dry-run        # fetch + validate, write nothing
node Scraper/scraper.js --quiet          # summary only
```

Tuning via environment variables (see `Scraper/algolia-config.js`):

| variable | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SCRAPE_CONCURRENCY` | `4` | parallel Algolia requests |
| `SCRAPE_RETRIES` | `5` | retries per request (exponential backoff) |
| `SCRAPE_MIN_CARS` | `1000` | abort instead of writing a suspiciously small file |
| `ALGOLIA_APP_ID` / `ALGOLIA_API_KEY` / `ALGOLIA_INDEX` | see above | credential override |

Output goes to `Scraper/unprocessed/<timestamp>-<count>.json`, which is exactly
what `npm run db:save` consumes.

## Safety behaviour

The scraper refuses to write output that would poison the database:

- any failed partition → exits non-zero and writes **nothing** (no partial files)
- fewer than `SCRAPE_MIN_CARS` cars → exits non-zero, writes nothing
- 401/403 from Algolia → tells you the key rotated instead of writing empty rows
- **type validation against the Prisma schema** → if any field would not satisfy
  `prisma.car.create()` (e.g. a spec value arriving as an object), the run exits
  non-zero and writes nothing, naming the offending fields

That last check exists because an invalid type only surfaces *inside*
`save-to-db.js`, after thousands of rows have already been inserted, and leaves
the import half-done. Catching it before the file is written means `npm run
scrape` either imports cleanly or fails loudly with nothing to clean up.

## Repairing an existing scrape file

If you have a file produced by an older mapper (for example one containing
`motorsTrim: { id: null, name: null }`, which makes Prisma throw
`Expected String or Null, provided Object`), you can fix it in place instead of
re-scraping:

```bash
node Scraper/repair-scrape.js Scraper/unprocessed/<file>.json --dry-run   # report only
node Scraper/repair-scrape.js Scraper/unprocessed/<file>.json             # fix in place
```

It only rewrites fields whose runtime type does not match the schema, so it is
safe to run repeatedly, and afterwards `npm run db:save` will complete.

## Importing a scrape into the database

`npm run db:save` (or the equivalent step inside `npm run scrape`) reads every
`.json` in `Scraper/unprocessed/`, imports it, then moves the file to
`Scraper/processed/`.

It is batched. The original version issued a `findUnique` and then an
`insert`/`update` **per car** - about 70,000 round trips for a 34.5k-car scrape,
which is why the step dominated the pipeline runtime. It now:

1. reads and parses every queued file,
2. loads the existing rows those files could affect with a few chunked
   `findMany` calls (not the whole table),
3. decides insert / update / skip in memory,
4. bulk-inserts new cars and their history with `createMany`,
5. applies updates with a single `updateMany` per distinct change-set.

A car whose scraped data is identical to what is stored costs **no SQL at all**,
so a re-import that finds nothing new does not touch the database.

| 5,000 cars | before | after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| fresh insert | ~2 min | **2.8 s** |
| re-import, no changes | ~2 min | **~1 s** |
| 25 cars with changed price/mileage | ~2 min | **~4 s** |

That is ~0.55 ms/car inserting, so the full 117k-row table imports in well under
a minute instead of ~45.

### Behaviour is verified against the old implementation

`Scraper/diag/differential-save.js` runs the **pre-optimization** script (pulled
from git) and the new one over identical fixture files against separate throwaway
databases, then diffs every `Car` and `CarHistory` row. Current status: all
scenarios identical, covering

- newer data wins; prices and mileage update and are recorded in `CarHistory`
- a brand-new listing appearing later is inserted
- nulls never overwrite stored values
- an older (or equally old) file changes nothing
- `1`/`0` and `"2016"`-style values are coerced to the right column types

Two deliberate differences from the old script, both of which were bugs:

- **Unknown keys in a scraped record are ignored.** The old script spread the
  whole record into Prisma, so any extra field (e.g. `categorySlugPaths`, `site`)
  made the import throw `Unknown argument` and abort.
- **`1`/`0` are accepted for Boolean columns** instead of erroring with
  `Expected Boolean or Null, provided Int`.

Also note `last_updated` still means **"when this car's data last changed"** - an
unchanged car keeps its old timestamp rather than being refreshed on every run.
That matters: `last_updated` currently has only ~141 distinct values across
117k cars, so bumping unchanged rows would collapse thousands of cars onto one
timestamp and erase the history.

Files move to `processed/` only after their rows are committed, and re-running is
safe: rows already at or newer than the file's timestamp are skipped.

## If the key rotates

The key above is the public search key every browser receives, so it is not a
secret - but Dubizzle can rotate it. Symptom: the scraper exits with
`Algolia rejected the credentials (403)`.

To refresh it: open <https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/> in a real
browser, then in DevTools → Network look for a request to `algolia.net` or
`algolia.dubizzle.com` and copy the `x-algolia-api-key` header (and
`x-algolia-application-id`). Then either export it:

```bash
$env:ALGOLIA_API_KEY="..."; npm run scrape
```

or copy `Scraper/algolia-credentials.example.json` to
`Scraper/algolia-credentials.json` (git-ignored) and fill it in.

## Files

| file | role |
| --- | --- |
| `Scraper/scraper.js` | the scraper (Algolia) |
| `Scraper/algolia-config.js` | credentials, filter, tuning |
| `Scraper/algolia-mapper.js` | Algolia hit → DB/export field mapping |
| `Scraper/save-to-db.js` | import `unprocessed/*.json` → SQLite (batched) |
| `Scraper/test-save-to-db.js` | regression test for the import (throwaway db) |
| `Scraper/repair-scrape.js` | fix schema-invalid fields in an existing scrape file |
| `Scraper/test.js` | mapper unit tests + live API smoke test |
| `Scraper/car-data-embed.js` | gzip/base64 embed + validation for index.html |
| `Scraper/migrate-template.js` | convert bootstrap.html to the compressed format |
| `update-index-html-with-new-cars.js` | build index.html (compress + embed) |
| `export-cars-from-db.js` | export all cars from the DB |
| `Scraper/legacy/scraper-html-2025.js` | the old Playwright scraper, kept for reference |
| `Scraper/legacy/bootstrap-inline-json.html` | pre-compression template, kept for reference |
| `Scraper/diag/` | investigation scripts + archived page snapshots (git-ignored) |

## Data notes

- `added` = when the car was **posted** to Dubizzle.
- `createdAt` = when the seller's **account** was created.
- `sellerType` is decoded from the site's codes (`OW` → Owner, `DL` → Dealer,
  `RL` → Rental, `AG` → Agent, `BR` → Business, `DS` → Dealership/Certified Pre-Owned).
- `spec` is stored without the trailing " Specs" (`GCC Specs` → `GCC`).
- List-valued fields (`badges`, `extras`, `technicalFeatures`) are split into one
  array entry per item, preserving names that contain commas inside parentheses
  (e.g. `Anti-Lock Brakes (ABS)`).

---

## index.html: why the car data is compressed

`index.html` is a single self-contained file, so the car data has to live inside
it. As raw JSON the whole database is **~117 MB**, over GitHub Pages' 100 MB
per-file limit - which is why the export used to be capped at 70,000 cars
(~88 MB) and why recent listings could be missing from the page.

The data is now **gzip-compressed and base64-encoded** into a
`<script type="application/octet-stream" id="car-data">` block, and inflated in
the browser with the built-in `DecompressionStream` API - no library, no extra
network request:

| | raw JSON | gzip + base64 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| all 117,456 cars | 115.6 MB | **20.4 MB** |
| old 70,000-car cap | 69.7 MB | 12.4 MB |

So the cap is gone: the page now ships **every** car (~20 MB, decoding in well
under a second) with room to grow to roughly 500k listings before the limit
matters again.

### How the pieces fit

```
export-cars-from-db.js            -> all-cars-from-db.json   (every car, no cap)
update-index-html-with-new-cars.js -> index.html             (compresses + embeds)
bootstrap.html                     -> the template (no data, ~61 KB)
```

`bootstrap.html` is the template and holds the loader, not the data. The build
step injects the compressed payload. Both are validated before writing: the build
refuses to publish a page that lost dashboard code, has a malformed payload, or
would be left with an empty dataset.

Run it with:

```bash
npm run export:all     # dump the DB (all cars)
npm run sync:index     # compress + rebuild index.html
```

Requires a browser with `DecompressionStream`: Chrome/Edge 80+, Firefox 113+,
Safari 16.4+ (released 2023). Older browsers show a clear error instead of an
empty table.

### If you edit the template

`bootstrap.html` no longer contains inline JSON. If you ever restore a copy that
does, convert it once with:

```bash
npm run migrate:template        # adds --dry-run to preview
```

That rewrites `const jsonData = {...}` into the `loadCarData()` loader, wraps the
data-dependent initialisation in an async block, and neutralises the template's
`tableData = jsonData.Car` assignments (which would otherwise overwrite the
loaded records with the empty placeholder).

